\subsection{Track merging correction}%ie, track merging

If two tracks have small separations in pseudorapidity and azimuth, they are more difficult to resolve because hits from each particle may not be resolved by the TPC.  Therefore, if the particles have nearly identical momenta, their tracks are not distinguished.  This effect is called track merging and causes an artificial dip in the raw correlations centered at (\dphi,\deta) = (0,0).  Another similar effect is evident in the TPC from the crossing of high-\pT tracks.  While the hits are not lost in this case, one track will lose hits near the crossing point and this particle's track gets split into two shorter tracks.  Shorter tracks are less likely to meet the track quality cuts in \Cref{PartID} because they have fewer hits.  This effect causes artificial dips near (\dphi,\deta) = (0,0) but slightly displaced in \dphi.  The location of these dips in \dphi is dependent on the relative helicities of the trigger and associated particles.  The helicity is given by
\begin{equation}
 h = -\frac{qB}{|qB|}
\end{equation}
where q is the charge of the particle and B is the magnetic field \cite{galliard}.

A method for correcting high-\pT triggered di-hadron correlations was developed in \cite{galliard,Bombara:2008zza}, building on a method for correcting for track merging in studies of Hanbury-Brown-Twiss correlations \cite{Adams:2004yc}.  

\subsubsection{Correction for unidentified hadrons}

\Fref{TrackMerginghhCorr} shows a sample of raw di-hadron correlations between unidentified hadrons for all four helicity combinations in \Au events at \sNNsixtytwo.  When the helicities of the trigger and associated particles are the same, the percentage of overlapping hits is greater.  Track pairs are lost whether the pair is part of the combinatorial background or part of the signal so the magnitude of the dip is greater when the background is greater.  \Au collisions were chosen to demonstrate the effect because the background is largest for these collisions, making the effect easy to see.


