\Fref{SampleCorrected} shows a sample correlation after detector acceptance and track merging corrections.  To study the \ns of di-hadron correlations, the two components are separated and the yield, number of particles in each within limits on \ptassoc and \pttrig, is studied.  As discussed in \Cref{PreviousCorrelationResults}, the \ridge was previously observed to be roughly independent of \deta within the acceptance of the STAR TPC while the \jlc is confined to $|$\deta$|$ $<$ 0.75.

\subsection{Extraction of the \jl yield}

The raw correlation consists of a combinatorial background modulated by elliptic flow, \vtwo, and the signal, which consists of the \jlc and the \ridge on the \ns.  There are two methods used to extract the \jly from the raw correlation.  The first method uses different projections in \dphi, the fact that the \jlc is not present at large \deta, and the fact that the combinatorial background and the \ridge are both roughly independent of \deta to isolate the \jlc as a function of \dphi.  The second method involves taking a projection in \deta for $-1<$ \dphi $<1$ and subtracting a constant background to determine the \jlc as a function of \deta.  Elliptic flow, \vtwo, does not lead to a systematic error on the \jly using either of these methods because \vtwo is roughly independent of \deta within the STAR acceptance \cite{Back:2004mh,Back:2004zg}.  Previous studies in \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred have found these methods to be consistent at high-\pT \cite{STAROANAPAPER}, with some indications that there may be greater deviations at lower \pT \cite{JOERNRIDGEPAPER}.  Both methods are described in greater detail below.

\subsubsection{\dphi method}
To determine the \jly, \njet, the projection of the distribution of particles $\frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta}$ is taken in two different ranges in pseudorapidity:
\begin{eqnarray}
  \frac{dY_{ridge}}{d\Delta\phi} & = & \frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \int\limits_{-1.75}^{-0.75} \frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} d\Delta\eta 
   + \frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \int\limits_{0.75}^{1.75} \frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} d\Delta\eta\\ \label{ridgeeqtn1}
\frac{dY_{jet+ridge}}{d\Delta\phi} &= &\frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \int\limits_{-0.75}^{0.75} \frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} d\Delta\eta\label{jeteqtn1}
\end{eqnarray}
where the former contains only the \ridge and the latter contains both the \jlc and the \ridge.    The \jl yield, \njet, is determined using bin counting in the region $-1<$ \dphi $<1$:
\begin{equation}
Y_{jet}^{\Delta\phi} = \int\limits_{-1}^{1} ( \frac{dY_{jet+ridge}}{d\Delta\phi} - \frac{0.75}{1}  \frac{dY_{ridge}}{d\Delta\phi} ) d\Delta\phi. \label{jetphieqtn}
\end{equation}\label{dphieqtn}
The factor in front of the second term is the ratio of the \deta width in the region containing the \jlc and the \ridge to the width of the region containing only the \ridge.  In practice because the data have been reflected about \dphi = 0 and \deta = 0, the projections in \dphi and \deta and the bin counting are done over half of the range and then scaled up to ensure that the errors are correct.

\subsubsection{\deta method}
In this method the projection of the distribution of particles $\frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta}$ on the \ns is taken:
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \frac{dN}{d\Delta\eta} = \frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \int\limits_{-1}^{1} \frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} d\Delta\phi.\label{jetetaeqtnA}
\end{equation}
This contains both a background due to combinatorics and the \ridge, both of which are independent of \deta.  The background is determined first by fitting a Gaussian signal plus a constant background, A, to \Eref{jetphieqtnA} and then using the background from that for background subtraction.  If statistics permitted, the background could be determined from  $|$\deta$|>$ 0.75, however, many of the correlations presented here have limited statistics and using a fit of the whole region to determine the background prevents statistical fluctuations from skewing the background subtraction.  The \jl yield is:
\begin{equation}
 Y_{jet}^{\Delta\eta}  = \frac{1}{N_{trigger}} \int\limits_{-1}^{1} (\frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} - A) d\Delta\phi.\label{jetphieqtnA}
\end{equation}
The yield is determined by bin counting in the range $|$\deta$|<1$.  To get the proper errors, the projection and the bin counting are also done over half of the range and scaled because the data have been reflected.  The statistical error on A from the fit is used to determine the error on the final yield by adding it in quadrature to the statistical error from bin counting.  The magnitudes of these two errors are comparable.

