\subsection{Extraction of the \ridge yield}

There is a large combinatorial background which is dependent on \dphi.  The raw signal has a background due to particles correlated indirectly with each other in azimuth due to their correlation with the reaction plane. This random background is estimated by
\begin{equation}
\frac{dY_{bkgd}}{d\phi} = B (1 + 2 \langle v_2^{trigger}\rangle \langle v_2^{associated}\rangle \cos(2\Delta\phi) )\label{bkgdeqtn}
\end{equation}
where \vtwo is the second order harmonic in a Fourier expansion of the momentum anisotropy relative to the reaction plane \cite{Bielcikova:2003ku}.  The derivation of this parameterization of the background is dependent on the assumption that di-jet production is not correlated with the reaction plane so that the correlation between particles in the jet and random particles in event are not correlated in azimuth through a mutual correlation with the reaction plane.  Systematic errors come from the errors on B, $\langle v_2^{trigger}\rangle$ and $\langle v_2^{associated}\rangle$.  \vtwo is determined from independent measurements.  It is assumed that \vtwo is the same for events with a trigger particle as for minimum bias events and that \vtwo is independent of \deta.  For each data set $v_2(p_T)$ was fit in centrality bins to determine $\langle v_2^{trigger}\rangle$ and $\langle v_2^{associated}\rangle$.


\subsubsection{Measurements of \vtwo}
The best method for the determination of \vtwo depends on the collision system.  Since \vtwo is a momentum anisotropy in azimuth, the tracks produced in an event can be used to reconstruct the reaction plane.  \vtwo in \Eref{bkgdeqtn} is the azimuthal momentum anisotropy due to the collective flow of particles, however, there are other sources of azimuthal momentum anisotropy.  For particles above 1 \GeV, jets are the largest known non-flow source of momentum anisotropy.  Various methods for measuring \vtwo have different non-flow contributions.  The systematic error is determined by comparing these different methods.  Generally, the higher the multiplicity of an event and the greater the eccentricity of the overlap region, the easier it is to reconstruct the event plane.  Therefore that the event plane is more difficult to reconstruct in \Cu collisions.

For \Cu collisions, the upper limit for \vtwo was taken from a measurement of \vtwo using tracks from the FTPCs for reconstruction of the event plane.  \vtwo measured in \Cu using the event plane from the FTPCs is shown as a function of \pT and centrality in \Fref{v2CuCu62} for collisions at \sNNsixtytwo and in \Fref{v2CuCu200} for collisions at \sNNtwohundred \cite{Voloshin:2007af}.  These data were fit to a parameterization which was used for the subtraction of \vtwo using \Eref{bkgdeqtn}.  The fit parameters are given in \Tref{CuCuv2FitTable}.  Statistical errors on the fits are neglected in the determination of the error due to \vtwo since the systematic errors on \vtwo are several times larger than the statistical errors.  The event plane reconstructed from tracks measured in both of the FTPCs is expected to be less biased by jets since tracks in the FTPCs are separated by more than two units of pseudorapidity.  Particles produced in di-jets in \pp collisions do not exhibit long range correlations in pseudorapidity.  However, the observation of the \ridge in \AplusA collisions indicates that some azimuthal anisotropy may be present in \AplusA collisions which is not due to the collective flow of particles in a fluid, therefore this gives an upper limit on \vtwo.  The lower bound on the $v_2$ is either 0 for the 0-10\% centrality bin or 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{v_{2,centrality}^2-v_{2,0-10\%}^2\frac{M_{centrality}}{M_{0-10\%}}} 
\end{equation}
where M is the mean reference multiplicity, for the other bins.  The lower limit is based on the assumption that the measured \vtwo in central collisions is entirely due to non-flow and this non-flow scales with multiplicity \cite{Adams:2004wz,Aihong}.
