\vtwo for \lam, \xibary, and \omegabary baryons and \kaon mesons has been observed to follow quark scaling in \Au collisions at both \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred \cite{STARAuAuV0Flow} and in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred \cite{Shi:2008bs}.  The systematic errors on identified particle \vtwo in \Cu collisions are more difficult due to low statistics.  Quark scaling is assumed and the systematic error is estimated from unidentified hadron \vtwo.    For a hadron comprising $n_q$ quarks:
%3./2. * v2(dataset,centbin, 2./3.* pt, 0,range)
\begin{equation}
 v_2^{n_q} = \frac{n_q}{n_{\pi}} v_2( \frac{n_{\pi}}{n_q} p_T )
\end{equation}
where $n_{\pi}$ = 2.  It is assumed that unidentified hadron \vtwo is dominated by \pion \vtwo.





B is determined using the Zero Yield At Minimum (ZYAM) method \cite{STARLowPtAssoc2005,Back:2004zg}.  This method fixes B by assuming that there is no signal at the minimum of the correlation in azimuth.  For measurements with low statistics the statistical error on the minimum data point is a significant contribution to the error on the \ry.  This error is added in quadrature to the statistical error.

To determine the yield of the \ridge, \nridge, the di-hadron correlation $\frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta}$ is projected over the entire \deta region.  To minimize the effects of statistical fluctuations in the determination of the background, $\frac{dY_{bkgd}}{d\phi}$, \njet is subtracted after the projection in \deta:
\begin{equation}
Y_{Ridge} = 1/N_{trigger} \int\limits_{-1.75}^{1.75} \int\limits_{-1}^{1} (\frac{d^2N}{d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta} - \frac{dY_{bkgd}}{d\phi}) d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta - Y_{Jet}^{\Delta\eta}. 
\end{equation}
The integration over \dphi is done by bin counting.  \njeteta is used for the subtraction of \njet because it is less sensitive to the assumption that the \jlc is contained within $|$\deta$|<$ 0.75.  Sample correlations in azimuth after the projection over \deta are shown in \Fref{IntegratedSamples} for all data sets.  These correlations show the background level and shape for the high and low bounds on \vtwo.

